Gorgias: persuasion, power, and living well (@greek-gorgias: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @greek-socratic-inquiry, #source @greek-gorgias-text, #philosophy @greek-xenophon, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-xenophon) { r_5G884J0SFXCP4YRBFF5C5YSYD2
**Read:** [Gorgias, translated by Benjamin Jowett](../sources/Gorgias-Jowett.lingua#r_3D3G4G9SE2PC96ZWFB8M6ZZR0V). Read in three sittings: the exchange with Gorgias about rhetoric; the exchange with Polus about power and injustice; the extended exchange with Callicles about nature, convention, desire, and a good life. The closing myth belongs to the dialogue's presentation and should be compared with its arguments. First selections can use approximately 447a–461b, 466a–481b, and 482c–508c before completing the work.

**Preparation:** [an argument and an objection](../foundations/Read-an-argument.lingua#r_4GMQCKPBF9BCSKDK0KPN013EBN). [Crito](Crito.lingua#r_54N10WGKCSCKW6XM202PCG7ZDH) helps with injustice and action; [Meno](Meno.lingua#r_6NC59NWAQ6A7YZYBJ3CG12SCTN) helps distinguish a successful result from knowledge of its basis. Recover those distinctions alongside the reading if entering here.

Begin by distinguishing producing conviction from teaching what is true. Ask whether rhetoric must lack knowledge, can be used with it, or can help an audience recognise a good argument. Plato gives different interlocutors different commitments; avoid merging their answers into one generic opponent.

When power is discussed, distinguish doing what one presently wants from securing what is actually good for oneself. Reconstruct the argument that doing injustice harms its agent. Then identify why Callicles would resist its view of the good rather than treating his disagreement as a failure to understand a word.

**Practice:** compare two explanations of a public decision: one secures assent, the other supplies reasons whose truth an audience can examine. Give a case where they coincide and a case where they separate. Apply the distinction to a technical claim or an advertisement without assuming that persuasive language is automatically false.

**Check:** reconstruct an argument from each of two interlocutors, including the commitment that drives their disagreement. Defend one objection to Socrates' reasoning and a possible response.

**Next:** [A second author's Socrates](Xenophon.lingua#r_386T9BE05SXJZK8E0NX7TAYJK8). **Further depth:** [Aristotle on living well](../aristotle/Living-well-and-character.lingua#r_21YB00AWNRYQ0X2SQ8H0RFRW65); [Epicurus on desire](../schools/Epicurus.lingua#r_6KHZKEFRKY1J18Z7TSRAJABZQP). **Return:** [First reading path](Socratic-inquiry.lingua#r_24EDXVJN0ACP2G204JK3KCHW79) · [Ancient Greek philosophy](../Ancient-Greek-Philosophy.lingua#r_464ZHR7ZYC234A0XX2QEW47VB3).
